# Haryana Environment, Wildlife and Protected Areas

> Haryana is one of India's least forested states, yet it has two national
> parks, eight wildlife sanctuaries, two Ramsar wetlands and a Shivalik-to-
> Aravalli belt of hill forest. Exams ask "which district is Kalesar in",
> "which sanctuary is in Sirsa" and "name the state bird and animal". This
> lesson organises every protected area by district so you can answer the
> typical match-the-following in seconds.

## 1. State Symbols

| Symbol | Haryana's choice |
| --- | --- |
| State animal | Blackbuck |
| State bird | Black francolin (locally called kala teetar) |
| State flower | Lotus |
| State tree | Bodhi tree (peepal, Ficus religiosa) |

Exam trap: the blackbuck is the Haryana state animal; the chinkara
(Indian gazelle) is bred at a centre in Bhiwani district but is not the
state animal. The Bodhi tree and the peepal are the same species.

## 2. National Parks

Haryana has two national parks (official Haryana Forest Department list).

| Park | District | Notified | Key facts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Kalesar National Park | Yamunanagar | 8 Dec 2003 (the sanctuary around it dates from 1996) | Shivalik foothills, sal forest; adjoins Simbalbara National Park (Himachal Pradesh) and Rajaji National Park (Uttarakhand); leopard, elephant, sambar, red junglefowl |
| Sultanpur National Park | Gurugram (near Farrukhnagar) | Bird sanctuary 1972, national park July 1991 | About 1.4 sq km wetland; more than 100 migratory bird species each winter; Ramsar site |

[definition]
**Kalesar** is the only Haryana national park in the Shivalik foothills and
forms an elephant and leopard corridor with the Rajaji landscape in
Uttarakhand. **Sultanpur** is the smaller, wetland park in Gurugram, a
short drive from Delhi. Ask yourself "hills or lake" to separate them.
[/definition]

The wildlife sanctuary at Kalesar was notified in 1996 and the national
park in 2003; exams sometimes ask which came first.

## 3. Wildlife Sanctuaries and Reserves

| Sanctuary | District | Notified |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Kalesar Wildlife Sanctuary | Yamunanagar | 1996 |
| Bir Shikargah Wildlife Sanctuary | Panchkula | 1987 |
| Chhilchhila Wildlife Sanctuary | Kurukshetra | 1986 |
| Nahar Wildlife Sanctuary | Rewari | 1987 |
| Abubshahar Wildlife Sanctuary | Sirsa | 1987 |
| Bhindawas Wildlife Sanctuary | Jhajjar | 1986 |
| Khaparwas Wildlife Sanctuary | Jhajjar | 1987 |
| Khol Hi-Raitan Wildlife Sanctuary | Panchkula | 2004 |

Two **conservation reserves** appear in the official list: **Saraswati
Conservation Reserve** (Kaithal and Kurukshetra, 2007) and **Bir Bara Ban**
(Jind, 2007). The list was published in 2021, so later additions are
possible.

- **Abubshahar** (Sirsa) is the biggest sanctuary by area, about 11,530 ha, in the dry western plains; **Chhilchhila** (Kurukshetra) is the smallest, about 29 ha.
- **Jhajjar district has two** sanctuaries (Bhindawas and Khaparwas) and **Panchkula has two** (Bir Shikargah and Khol Hi-Raitan).
- Breeding centres noted in the state list include blackbuck at Pipli, chinkara at Kairu (Bhiwani) and a pheasant centre at Morni.

## 4. Ramsar Sites

[example]
Haryana has **two Ramsar wetlands**, both designated on **25 May 2021**:
Sultanpur National Park (1.43 sq km, Gurugram) and Bhindawas Wildlife
Sanctuary (4.11 sq km, Jhajjar). A question "which Haryana protected area
is both a national park and a Ramsar site?" has one answer: Sultanpur.
Bhindawas is a sanctuary, not a national park.
[/example]

Peter Jackson, a wildlife conservationist, alerted Prime Minister Indira
Gandhi in 1970 to the need to protect the Sultanpur lake. She directed
Haryana's Chief Minister, Bansi Lal, to take action, and the site was
declared a sanctuary in 1972.

## 5. Forests, Hills and Biodiversity

Haryana's forest cover is among the lowest of any Indian state. The
2013 figures cited in references are about 3.6 percent forest cover and
2.9 percent tree cover outside forests (6.5 percent combined), and news
reports on a later Forest Survey of India report put forest plus tree cover
near 6.7 percent. The figure changes with each two-yearly **India State of
Forest Report (ISFR)**, so learn only the idea: very low and below the
national average.

| Region | Forest features |
| --- | --- |
| Shivalik foothills (Panchkula, Yamunanagar) | Morni Hills, Kalesar, Khol Hi-Raitan; sal and mixed forest, leopards and pheasants |
| Aravalli (Gurugram, Faridabad, Mahendragarh, Rewari, Bhiwani) | Dry thorn and scrub forest; Mangar Bani, Nahar, Khaparwas |
| Northern plains | Chhilchhila and the Saraswati reserve with wetland birds |
| West (Sirsa, Hisar, Jind) | Dry forest and grassland; Abubshahar and Bir Bara Ban |

**Mangar Bani** (Faridabad) is a roughly 5,000 ha sacred grove on the Aravalli
ridge, protected by local belief in the saint Gudariya Das. As of 2021, it
was awaiting full legal protection, so avoid the phrase "protected forest"
in your answer.

## 6. Pollution and the Regulator

The **Haryana State Pollution Control Board (HSPCB)** enforces the Water
(Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 and the Air (Prevention
and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981 in the state. It sets standards, grants
consent to industries and monitors water and air quality.

Main environmental concerns in Haryana:

- Winter air pollution in NCR districts such as Gurugram, Faridabad and Sonipat, where paddy-straw burning and urban emissions add up.
- River pollution in the Yamuna and the drains that reach it, and contamination of the Ghaggar by drain water.
- Falling groundwater in many blocks, which affects farming and wetlands alike.

[summary]
State animal: blackbuck. Bird: black francolin. Flower: lotus. Tree: Bodhi.
National parks: Kalesar (Yamunanagar, 2003) and Sultanpur (Gurugram, 1991).
Sanctuaries: Kalesar, Bir Shikargah and Khol Hi-Raitan (both Panchkula),
Chhilchhila (Kurukshetra), Nahar (Rewari), Abubshahar (Sirsa),
Bhindawas and Khaparwas (Jhajjar). Ramsar: Sultanpur and Bhindawas, 25 May
2021. Forest cover is very low. HSPCB works under the Water Act 1974 and Air
Act 1981.
[/summary]
